    {
      "filename": "Carrerot House & Benoist House (35781870181).jpg",
      "title": "File:Carrerot House & Benoist House (35781870181).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Carrerot_House_%26_Benoist_House_%2835781870181%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carrerot_House_%26_Benoist_House_(35781870181).jpg",
      "width": 6000,
      "height": 4000,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Dennis G. Jarvis",
      "description": "Looking down one of the main streets at the fortress.\nCarrerot House (first house), he was a merchant, commissary for naval conscription and for the colonial regular troops in 1749 at Louisbourg.\nWhen he arrived Carrerot was employed as inspector of fortifications, in 1718 at Louisbourg. He was a storekeeper at Louisbourg in 1724. \nAfter the fall of Louisbourg in 1745 the Carrerots took refuge in France, where their fourth [fourteenth] child was born. He was back on Ile Royale in 1749, but some months after his arrival he died, leaving his family penniless. \nNext House:\nBenoist House (house with red shutters) was another house full of children, for Lieutenant Pierre Benoit (c 1695-1763) had seven children in his two marriages. One died young killed with her mother by smallpox. Genoist was hardly rich, but military families had to maintain an air of refinement - in 1743, while Benoist was serving at Port Toulouse down the coast, a charitable bequest was paying for his daughter’s education at the convent school of the Sister’s of Notre-Dame.\nThe convent, Louisbourg’s only formal school; taught devotions, craftwork and decorum to the daughters of the elite, but other children did not go uneducated. The literate minority could instruct their children at home with alphabets and primers, and all children were expected to learn prayers and catechism. Soon after puberty, boys were apprenticed into trades and professions and learned the skills they needed on the job. At about this age, both sexes began to enter adult society, but parents would retain responsibility for their children for many years. Benoit’s care for his children resulted in one advantageous marriage: the signing of the marriage contract of Benoit’s daughter Genevive to the nephew of his neighbour Carrerot in 1753 brought together the military and commercial elite of the colony.\n\nNext is the L’Epée Royale Café (previous picture)",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 14 June 2017, 12:16",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "fortress-of-louisbourg-wp",
